EXT. PIGEON LOFT - ROOFTOP - EVENING

A rickety wooden coop on a tenement roof. Wire mesh. Birds shifting and cooing. Laundry lines snap in the wind. Behind it, a skyline washed orange.

AUGUSTINE "AUGIE" MBEKI (70s, flat cap, hands gnarled as roots) kneels at the coop door feeding corn from a coffee can.

RADEK NOWAK (40s, his son-in-law, hospital badge still clipped on) stands behind him, jacket slung over a shoulder.

Beside the water trough, ZUZANNA "ZUZU" NOWAK (12, Radek's daughter, school uniform, backpack at her feet) holds a gray pigeon against her chest.

NADEEM ABBASI (30s, neighbor, tool belt) leans on the stairwell door frame with a beer.

RADEK
Augie, we have to talk about Friday.

AUGIE
Nothing to talk about.

RADEK
The building's being sold.

AUGIE
I know what the building is.

NADEEM
Council gave the tenants sixty days.

AUGIE
Nadeem. Drink your beer.

NADEEM
I'm only saying.

ZUZU
Grandpa, what happens to the birds?

Augie tips the last corn from the can. He closes the coop door with a soft click.

AUGIE
They fly home.

ZUZU
But home is here.

AUGIE
Then they'll come back and find the roof empty.

Silence. Radek puts a hand on Augie's shoulder. Augie does not shrug it off.

RADEK
We'll find another roof.

AUGIE
Where? In Queens? They'd never find their way.

NADEEM
Augie, they've done it before. You said so.

AUGIE
Mm.

Zuzu lifts the pigeon high. It flaps once, twice, and lifts out of her hands into the orange sky. They all watch. It circles the roof. Settles on the antenna.

ZUZU
Grandpa. Look.

Augie nods slowly. He tilts his cap back.

AUGIE
That one's called Hope. Your grandmother named her.
